<section class="shell band">
  <p class="eyebrow">Blog &middot; October 2026</p>
  <h1>Why walk and talk therapy works</h1>
  <div class="answer">
    <p>People who have sat in a therapy room and found it hard to speak often assume the problem
    is them. Sometimes the problem is the room. Two chairs facing each other, sustained eye
    contact, and an expectation that you will produce words about something painful is a
    demanding setup, and for some nervous systems it is the wrong one.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="shell band">
  <h2>What changes on a trail</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>You are side by side, not face to face.</strong> Direct sustained eye contact is
    a lot of social load, and for people whose early relationships were unsafe it can read as
    scrutiny rather than attention. Walking removes it without removing the relationship. Plenty
    of people say the hardest sentence of their life while looking at a trail.</li>

    <li><strong>Your body has somewhere to put the activation.</strong> Talking about something
    difficult mobilises energy. In a chair there is nowhere for it to go, so it shows up as a
    racing heart, a tight chest or the urge to leave. Walking gives that energy an outlet while
    the conversation continues, which is often the difference between staying with something and
    shutting down.</li>

    <li><strong>Silence stops being awkward.</strong> A pause in a room is conspicuous and most
    people fill it. A pause while walking is just walking. Some of the useful parts of a session
    happen in those minutes, and the trail makes them easy to leave alone.</li>

    <li><strong>The pace is honest.</strong> People slow down when they get near something that
    matters, and speed up when they want past it. That is visible outdoors in a way it is not in
    a chair, and it is worth noticing out loud.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="band band--night">
  <div class="shell">
    <h2>Who it is not for</h2>
    <div class="split">
      <div>
        <p>Outdoor sessions are not more therapeutic by default, and saying so would be selling
        something. A trail is a public place. Complete confidentiality is not available on one,
        and anyone who tells you otherwise is not being careful with you.</p>
        <p>If what you need to talk about is something you would not want a passing stranger to
        catch three words of, that conversation belongs indoors or online. That is not a lesser
        option; it is the right room for that material.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>It is also the wrong format if walking is painful, if the heat is a medical problem
        for you, or if being outdoors is itself activating. None of that needs explaining or
        justifying in the consult.</p>
        <p>Plenty of people do some of both, and move between them depending on what the week
        holds.</p>
