</section>

<section class="shell band faq">
  <h2>Questions about walking sessions</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>Does walk and talk therapy work as well as sitting in an office?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>For some people, better, and for some people not at all. It is a format rather than a
      method: the same approaches apply either way. What changes is how easy it is to stay in
      the conversation, and that varies by person and by what is being talked about.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Who is walk and talk therapy not a good fit for?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Anyone whose material is too sensitive to risk being overheard in a public place,
      anyone for whom walking or heat is a problem, and anyone who finds being outdoors
      activating rather than settling. It is also not the right starting point when someone is
      in acute crisis.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Do we talk the whole time?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>No, and that is part of why it works. Stretches of walking without speaking are normal
      and are not a sign the session is going badly. The quiet often does more than the talking
      does.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Is walking outside just a nicer version of a normal session?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>It is a different set of trade-offs rather than an upgrade. You gain side-by-side
      positioning, movement and easier silence. You give up privacy, climate control and the
      ability to do anything that needs you still and undistracted. Which matters more depends
      on the work.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
